# Spring-on-Exeris COMPAT MODE + NATIVE PERSISTENCE
# (targets/exeris-spring-runtime-app-comp-native).
#
# The missing cell of the two-axis grid. Before this arm the grid read:
#
#                       ORM over compat-datasource      kernel-native repositories
#   web compat          spring-on-exeris                 —                        <- unmeasured
#   web pure            spring-on-exeris-pure            spring-on-exeris-pure-native
#
# and the empty cell was the combination a Spring team would actually want: keep
# @RestController, swap the repositories. It is now filled.
#
# PRIMARY RESULT, ALREADY OBTAINED — the axes are NOT coupled. Verified 2026-08-07 against a
# seeded Postgres: TransactionalExecutor resolves correctly under ExerisCompatDispatcher, both
# contracts return 200, and the bodies are byte-identical to every other arm
# (heavy sha256/16 82f9bcdf2852bd5e, light 615734f3df404338 — the same values
# tools/preflight-ladder-arms.sh measured on all four ladder arms). This mattered because the
# kernel provider scope has NOT historically covered all of compat mode: the compat security
# filter ran outside it until runtime PR #48, and flow-worker virtual threads still do.
#
# PRIMARY PAIRING: spring-on-exeris-pure-native__spring-on-exeris-comp-native.
# Those two targets are built from the same POM — `diff` of the two jars' BOOT-INF/lib listings
# is empty — and differ by exactly one property, exeris.runtime.web.mode=compatibility. That
# makes it the only single-variable measurement of the Pure-vs-Compat WEB axis in this repo.
# It is classified non_eligible by strict gate G3 (mode pure vs compat) BY DESIGN: it is a
# compat-family overhead measurement per CLAUDE.md ("compat/ — pure-mode vs compatibility-mode
# overhead measurements; results from the two modes must be stored separately and labeled"),
# not a cross-target comparative claim. Store it accordingly.
#
# DO NOT pair against spring-on-exeris (app-comp) expecting a persistence-axis reading. That
# pair passes G3 — both are mode=compat — and is nonetheless the more confounded of the two:
# app-comp is still on Spring Boot 3.5.14 and Jackson 2 (com.fasterxml), outside the 2026-08-06
# alignment, and additionally carries Spring Security and the flow stack. Gate eligibility is
# not fairness.
#
# PORT 9007: the first free slot. 9000-9006 and 9010-9013 are taken
# (runtime/drivers/target-asset-matrix.json), and 9007 must stay disjoint from 9006 so this arm
# can co-reside with spring-on-exeris-pure-native, the arm it is paired against.
#
# CONTRACT SCOPE: heavy and light, same as the sibling arms.
TARGET_ID=spring-on-exeris-comp-native
HEALTH_URL=http://localhost:9007/health
HEALTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90

SPRING_ON_EXERIS_COMP_NATIVE_PORT=${SPRING_ON_EXERIS_COMP_NATIVE_PORT:-9007}

SPRING_JAVA_OPTS=${SPRING_JAVA_OPTS:-}
# --enable-preview is mandatory: kernel classes are compiled with preview features
# (class file 70.65535) and ExerisRuntimeLifecycle fails to start without it. Confirmed the hard
# way on this arm's first boot, 2026-08-07: without it the context dies in
# ExerisRuntimeLifecycle.start with UnsupportedClassVersionError on SubsystemOrchestrator.
# -Dspring.classformat.ignore=true keeps Spring's classpath scan off those preview classes.
# Appended AFTER SPRING_JAVA_OPTS so heap/JFR settings win. Identical to the sibling arms.
EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS=${EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS:-'--add-opens java.base/sun.nio.ch=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens java.base/java.io=ALL-UNNAMED --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --enable-preview -Dspring.classformat.ignore=true'}

# Required by runtime/drivers/start-target.sh: the start command ends with
# `echo $! > "$EXTERNAL_PID_FILE"`. Unset, that redirect targets an empty path and bash reports
# `line 1: : No such file or directory` AFTER the JVM has already launched — start-target.sh
# then reports the target as failed while an orphaned process keeps holding the port.
EXTERNAL_PID_FILE=${EXTERNAL_PID_FILE:-/tmp/exeris-${USER:-unknown}-spring-on-exeris-comp-native.pid}

# No SPRING_DATASOURCE_* exports, exactly as in the pure-native arm: there is no Spring
# DataSource and no JPA here. The kernel reads EXERIS_DB_* through exeris.runtime.persistence.*
# in application.properties.
EXTERNAL_START_CMD='EXERIS_PORT="$SPRING_ON_EXERIS_COMP_NATIVE_PORT" EXERIS_DB_JDBC_URL="$EXERIS_DB_JDBC_URL" EXERIS_DB_USERNAME="$EXERIS_DB_USERNAME" EXERIS_DB_PASSWORD="$EXERIS_DB_PASSWORD" EXERIS_DB_POOL_MIN_SIZE="$EXERIS_DB_POOL_MIN_SIZE" EXERIS_DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE="$EXERIS_DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE" ${SERVER_CPU_AFFINITY:+taskset -c ${SERVER_CPU_AFFINITY} }java $SPRING_JAVA_OPTS $EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS -jar targets/exeris-spring-runtime-app-comp-native/target/spring-benchmark-app-comp-native-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar > /tmp/exeris-spring-on-exeris-comp-native-9007.log 2>&1 & echo $! > "$EXTERNAL_PID_FILE"'
